WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.320
Euler continued to
investigate properties

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.000
of numbers, specifically the
distribution of prime numbers.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.110
One important
function he defined

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:12.860
is called the phi function.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:16.030
It measures the
breakability of a number.

00:00:16.030 --> 00:00:18.940
So given a number, say
n, it outputs how many

00:00:18.940 --> 00:00:22.480
integers are less than
or equal to n that do not

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.060
share any common factor with n.

00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.300
For example, if we want
to find the phi of 8,

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.950
we look at all
values from 1 to 8,

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:33.780
then we count how many
integers 8 does not

00:00:33.780 --> 00:00:36.200
share a factor
greater than 1 with.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:39.270
Notice 6 is not counted
because it shares a factor of 2

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:42.650
while 1, 3, 5, and 7 are all
counted because they only

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:44.665
share a factor of 1.

00:00:44.665 --> 00:00:48.680
Therefore phi of 8 equals 4.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.620
What's interesting is that
calculating the phi function

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:54.650
is hard, except in one case.

00:00:54.650 --> 00:00:56.030
Look at this graph.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:01:01.460
It is a plot of values of phi
over integers from 1 to 1,000.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.010
Now, notice any
predictable pattern?

00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:07.830
The straight line of
points along the top

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:10.900
represent all the prime numbers.

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:14.540
Since prime numbers have no
factors greater than one,

00:01:14.540 --> 00:01:20.140
the phi of any prime number,
p, is simply p minus 1.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.740
To calculate phi of
7, a prime number,

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.750
we count all integers
except 7, since none of them

00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:27.530
share a factor with 7.

00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:31.680
Phi of 7 equals 6.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:38.120
So if you're asked to find
phi of 21,377, a prime number,

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:44.100
you would only need to subtract
1 to get the solution, 21,376.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:48.240
Phi of any prime
is easy to compute.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.640
This leads to an interesting
result based on the fact

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.960
that the phi function
is also multiplicative.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:02:00.990
That is, phi a times b
equals phi a times phi b.

00:02:00.990 --> 00:02:05.250
If we know some number n is
the product of two primes, p1

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:09.600
and p2, then phi of
n is just the value

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.820
of phi for each prime
multiplied together,

00:02:12.820 --> 00:02:18.220
or p1 minus 1 times p2 minus 1.

